  "rendered_record_bank": [
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). Ecuador welcomed adoption as a milestone within a wider response, highlighted Kenya's willingness to consider leading and African countries' support, and emphasized Haiti's sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity (S/PV.9430, p. 8).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). China expressed caution on Chapter VII and described its position on adoption as constructive. It urged close consultation with Haiti to reach agreement and gain public support, and called for timely Council reporting (S/PV.9430, p. 3).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). Ecuador welcomed adoption as a milestone within a wider response, highlighted Kenya's willingness to consider leading and African countries' support, and emphasized Haiti's sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity (S/PV.9430, p. 8). China expressed caution on Chapter VII and described its position on adoption as constructive. It urged close consultation with Haiti to reach agreement and gain public support, and called for timely Council reporting (S/PV.9430, p. 3).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). Russia had no objection in principle but criticized invoking Chapter VII with unanswered requests about mission plans and withdrawal. It wanted Council approval of the concept of operations before it was presented as settled (S/PV.9430, p. 4).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). Ecuador welcomed adoption as a milestone within a wider response, highlighted Kenya's willingness to consider leading and African countries' support, and emphasized Haiti's sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity (S/PV.9430, p. 8). Russia had no objection in principle but criticized invoking Chapter VII with unanswered requests about mission plans and withdrawal. It wanted Council approval of the concept of operations before it was presented as settled (S/PV.9430, p. 4).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). China expressed caution on Chapter VII and described its position on adoption as constructive. It urged close consultation with Haiti to reach agreement and gain public support, and called for timely Council reporting (S/PV.9430, p. 3). Russia had no objection in principle but criticized invoking Chapter VII with unanswered requests about mission plans and withdrawal. It wanted Council approval of the concept of operations before it was presented as settled (S/PV.9430, p. 4). Both abstainers expressed reservations about Chapter VII. China urged consultation with Haiti and timely Council reporting, while Russia explicitly sought Council approval of the mission concept (S/PV.9430, pp. 3–4).",
    "On 2 October 2023, the Council adopted resolution 2699 by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. Ecuador and the United States submitted the draft (S/PV.9430, p. 2). Acting under Chapter VII, the Council authorized participating Member States that notify the Secretary-General to form and deploy the Multinational Security Support mission with a lead country, in coordination with Haiti (para. 1). The initial authorization lasts 12 months, with a nine-month review and financing through voluntary contributions and support (para. 1). The authorization supports Haitian police efforts to restore security and help create conditions for free and fair elections (para. 1). The Council requested a human rights compliance mechanism from participating States and called on the mission to establish oversight (paras. 10–11). It requested that mission leaders report through the Secretary-General every three months once the mission is operational on the ground (para. 18). Ecuador welcomed adoption as a milestone within a wider response, highlighted Kenya's willingness to consider leading and African countries' support, and emphasized Haiti's sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity (S/PV.9430, p. 8). China expressed caution on Chapter VII and described its position on adoption as constructive. It urged close consultation with Haiti to reach agreement and gain public support, and called for timely Council reporting (S/PV.9430, p. 3). Russia had no objection in principle but criticized invoking Chapter VII with unanswered requests about mission plans and withdrawal. It wanted Council approval of the concept of operations before it was presented as settled (S/PV.9430, p. 4). Both abstainers expressed reservations about Chapter VII. China urged consultation with Haiti and timely Council reporting, while Russia explicitly sought Council approval of the mission concept (S/PV.9430, pp. 3–4)."
